@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ito-rounding-order-nouns`

-- Income Tax (Rules for Rounding Amounts) Order, 5746-1986 (צו מס הכנסה (כללים לעיגול סכומים),
-- התשמ״ו–1986), made under s 120B(d) of the Income Tax Ordinance.
-- Quotations (`src:N`) are copied mechanically from line N of
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/regulations/income-tax-rules-for-rounding-amounts-order-5746-1986.he.wiki.txt
-- (sha256 3418c9bd...93b, Hebrew Wikisource revision 2925532 of 2025-05-13, retrieved 2026-10-08).
--
-- WHAT THE ORDER DOES. When an amount has been adjusted for the index under s 120B, each of its
-- ss 1-13 says to what multiple the adjusted amount is rounded and which way. Every section
-- has the same two limbs: (a) the adjusted amount "shall be rounded to the number of whole new
-- shekels, nearest, divisible by" N ("the round amount"); (b) "if the number of new shekels
-- exceeding the round amount is less than" N/2 it is "not taken into account", and "if it is
-- N/2 or more" N is added. N is 120, 12, 1, 1,200, 120, 10, 12, 10, 1, 120, 12, 1,200 and 10 in
-- ss 1 to 13. Ss 3 and 9 state the limb (b) as "less than half a new shekel is not counted;
-- half or more counts as a shekel", which is the same rule at N = 1. S 15 says rounding "does not
-- alter the amounts adjusted under s 120B for the purpose of the next index adjustment": the next
-- adjustment starts from the unrounded figure. S 14 is a 1985 transitional rule (inert here).
--
-- READING (a) AND (b) TOGETHER, NOT A FORK. Limb (a) says "nearest"; limb (b) then speaks of the
-- amount "exceeding the round amount" and tells which way to go. If the round amount is the
-- largest multiple not above the adjusted amount (the floor), (b) alone does all the work. If it
-- is the NEAREST multiple, the excess is negative whenever the nearest multiple is above, and (b)
-- adds nothing; where the nearest multiple is below, (b) adds a step from an excess of N/2 or
-- more, which cannot occur when it is the nearest, except at the tie. At the tie (excess exactly
-- N/2) "nearest" does not choose, and (b) says to go up either way. So every reading gives the
-- same figure for every amount; this module writes the floor reading and the tests below assert
-- that both nearest readings agree with it on a grid of amounts including every tie. The ties
-- and the sub-half amounts are exactly where a different reading would show.
--
-- AMOUNTS THAT ARE NOT WHOLE SHEKELS. The adjusted amount is a product of the index and has agorot.
-- The excess is compared with N/2 as it stands, not truncated: 59.9 over a round amount in
-- steps of 120 is less than 60 and is not counted; the "number of new shekels" 59 would give the
-- same answer, and the comparison 60.0 or more rounds up under either way of counting.
--
-- NOTHING HERE HAS A NUMBER OF ITS OWN except N (printed in the Order). The adjusted amounts are
-- inputs: they come from s 120B (row IL-03) and the Tax Authority.

§ `Rounding Order, 5746-1986`

§§ `Intro — the power and its source`
